;;; `std.closure/infer-closure` — closed **and** the requirement met.
;;;
;;; The control half of a pair. Its twin, `03_closed_and_owing.ein`, is the
;;; same program with one fact removed, and until M1d S1d.2.6 the two reported
;;; the **same verdict** — which was the pair's whole point, and why it exists.
;;; **They no longer do**: this one is `Solution` and the twin is
;;; `Open — owes 1`, which is the corner cashing out. See the twin's header for
;;; the argument; what matters here is that this half is the *discharged* one,
;;; so the pair still differs by exactly one fact and now differs by a word.
;;;
;;; ── The shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `r : A → B` is declared functional and total, so `infer-closure` derives
;;; `(__closed__ r)` and the hypothesis generator will never speculate an
;;; `r`-fact. The requirement `total` states — *every A has some B* — is met
;;; here by the one authored edge, so a closed `r` costs nothing: there was
;;; nothing left to guess.
;;;
;;; ── The three declarations, and they are different facts ──────────────────
;;;
;;; `(total r)`, `(total r is-a)` and `(total-owed r is-a)` are all here and
;;; none is redundant:
;;;
;;;   (total r)         arity 1 — the **marker** `infer-closure` reads, and
;;;                     the form `bijective-properties` derives
;;;   (total r is-a)    arity 2 — the **activator** of `std.algebra/total`,
;;;                     the scan that fires (false) on unreachability
;;;   (total-owed r is-a)
;;;                     arity 2 — the **obligation** dual of the same scan
;;;                     (M1d S1d.2.4). It asserts `(open r)`, derives nothing,
;;;                     and is what puts this program in the three-state
;;;                     read-out's scope at all: a program that states no
;;;                     obligation is judged by exhaustion, and that is what
;;;                     left this pair reporting one word for two states
;;;
;;; A rule is activated by a fact whose head is the rule's name and whose
;;; arity matches its parameter list, so the arity-1 marker does not activate
;;; the scan and the arity-2 activator does not satisfy `infer-closure`'s
;;; guard. Both are needed to have both behaviours, and a program with only
;;; one of them silently has only one.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(__closed__ r)` is engine vocabulary and not a thing a `:goal` can ask
;;; for directly, so `closed-observed` lifts it into an ordinary fact. Naming
;;; `closed-here` in the expectation closes it, which is what makes "the
;;; closure was derived, once, for `r` and nothing else" a claim rather than a
;;; hope.

(import std.algebra :symbols (functional total total-owed))
(import std.closure :symbols (infer-closure))

(relation is-a        T T :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation r           A B :why "{?1} is assigned {?2}")
(relation closed-here T   :why "{?1}'s extension was closed by infer-closure")

(is-a a1 A)
(is-a b1 B) (is-a b2 B)

;; The markers `infer-closure` reads (arity 1) …
(functional r)
(total      r)
;; … and the activator the unreachability scan needs (arity 2).
(total r is-a)
;; … and the obligation dual, which is what makes the verdict read `Solution`
;; here *by discharge* rather than by the generator having nothing to propose.
;; It is satisfied: `(r a1 b1)` below is the witness.
(total-owed r is-a)

;; `(__closed__ …)` is engine vocabulary; this lifts it into userspace so a
;; goal can name it.
(rule closed-observed ()
  :match  (__closed__ ?R)
  :assert (closed-here ?R)
  :priority 500
  :why    "{?R}'s extension is closed")

;; The witness. Delete this line and you have `03_closed_and_owing.ein`.
(r a1 b1)

(query
  :goal (closed-here ?R)
  ;; `r` is closed by derivation; the other two are scoped out by hand so the
  ;; blind generator is quiet and the verdict is about the requirement alone.
  :no-hypothesis (is-a closed-here)
  :expect (model (closed-here r)))
